    public int GetRandomInt() => (int)GetRandomFloat();

    public int GetRandomIntExclusiveMax() => UnityEngine.Random.Range(this.minValue, this.maxValue - 1);

    public override string ToString() => $"Range: [{this.minValue}, {this.maxValue}]";

    public void ShuffleValues(int[] array)
    {
        if (array == null || array.Length == 0) throw new ArgumentException("Array cannot be empty or null.");

        int n = this.maxValue - this.minValue + 1;
        if (n != array.Length) throw new ArgumentException($"Expected array of length {n} but got {array.Length} instead.");

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            array[i] = this.minValue + i;

        UnityEngine.Random.Shuffle(array);
    }

    public void ResetRange(int min, int max)
    {
        if (min > max) throw new ArgumentException("Minimum value must be less than or equal to maximum value.");
        this.minValue = min;
        this.maxValue = max;
    }
}
```
Note: This code is using Unity's `Random` class for random number generation and array shuffling, so it will work only in a Unity project context. Otherwise, you might need to use the `System.Random` class or an equivalent library. Also, please note that this implementation assumes that `UnityEngine.Random.Shuffle` method takes an integer array as input where each element is unique and within range [minValue, maxValue].
']

["Here is an example of how one might implement such a function in JavaScript using async/await syntax for asynchronous operations and Promises to handle potential errors gracefully. This version includes the above points, though it does not include all possibilities (e.g., handling large datasets efficiently or scaling) due to complexity.

```javascript
async function reduceAsync(array, initialValue, callback, timeout = 5000) {
  let result = initialValue;

  for (let i=0; i<array.length; i++) {
    try {
      // Assume the service is a simple asynchronous function
      const value = await withTimeout(timeout, () => externalService(array[i]));

      result = await callback(result, value);
    } catch (error) {
      console.log('Error occurred while processing data', error);
    }
  }

  return result;
}

function withTimeout(timeout, promiseFunction) {
  let complete = false;

  const timeoutPromise = new Promise((_, reject) => 
    setTimeout(() => complete || reject(new Error('Service timed out')), timeout));

  return Promise.race([promiseFunction(), timeoutPromise])
    .then(val => {complete = true; return val;});
}
```

In the above code:
- The `reduceAsync` function takes an array of values, an initial value, a callback that performs the reduction operation, and a timeout (defaults to 5 seconds). It iterates over each element in the array, passing it to an asynchronous service. If the service does not respond within the specified timeout, the Promise is rejected with a TimeoutError.
- The `withTimeout` function wraps any promise so that if it takes longer than the provided timeout to resolve, the operation will be cancelled and a TimeoutError will be thrown. It returns the result of the promise if it completes within the timeout; otherwise, it rejects with a TimeoutError after the specified timeout period.

Asynchronous error handling is performed using try/catch blocks: any errors that occur during service calls or callback execution are caught and logged to the console, but do not interrupt the overall function execution. 

This code assumes an external asynchronous function `externalService` which would be replaced with a real call to your actual service. It also assumes the existence of a function `callback` that performs the reduction operation. Memory usage is kept minimal by only processing one value at a time and using Promises for error handling. 

The scalability could be improved by spreading the array into chunks and process them in parallel (this would require a more complex implementation), or, if possible, by refactoring to work on streams of data from the start, so that you don't need to load all the data into memory at once. 

For increased scalability, it could be beneficial to implement a back-pressure strategy (e.g., using libraries such as async or Bottleneck), to prevent out-of-memory errors when dealing with large datasets. 

