using System.Text;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

public class BatchProcessor
{
    private readonly MD5 _md5Hash = MD5.Create();

    public void ProcessBatch(string[] strings, string outputPath)
    {
        try
        {
            if (strings == null || strings.Length == 0) return;

            using (var writer = new StreamWriter(outputPath))
            {
                foreach (var str in strings)
                {
                    byte[] data = _md5Hash.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(str));
                    StringBuilder sBuilder = new StringBuilder();

                    for (int i = 0; i < data.Length; i++)
                        sBuilder.Append(data[i].ToString("x2"));

                    writer.WriteLine($"{str}, {sBuilder}");
                }
            }
        }
        catch (IOException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error while writing to file: {ex.Message}");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Unexpected error: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, the `ProcessBatch` function receives an array of strings and a file path as parameters to write MD5 hashes. It uses an instance of the `MD5` hash object for each batch, avoiding recreating it multiple times. The file writing operation is done in a using block, which ensures that the stream is properly disposed when the operation is finished.

The error handling includes two types: a general catch-all clause to handle unexpected errors and specific handlers for `IOException` (file I/O exceptions) and other subtypes of `Exception`. These handlers log relevant messages in case an error occurs during file I/O operations or other unforeseen issues.

