['Here is an implementation based on Java ExecutorService and CompletionService:

```java
import java.nio.charset.*;
import java.security.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class HashProcessingService {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(HashProcessingService.class.getName());
    private static final int HASH_LENGTH = 16; // MD5 length in bytes
    private static final long MAX_MEMORY = 1L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 1 GB
    private static final int MAX_CONCURRENCY = (int) (MAX_MEMORY / HASH_LENGTH);

    private ExecutorService executor;
    private BitSet hashExists;
    private CountDownLatch latch;
    private long totalProcessingTime = 0;
    private int totalHashesProcessed = 0;

    public HashProcessingService() {
        executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(MAX_CONCURRENCY);
        hashExists = new BitSet(Integer.MAX_VALUE / HASH_LENGTH);
    }

    public void processBatch(List<String> strings) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        latch = new CountDownLatch(strings.size());

        long startTime;
        byte[] hashBytes;
        int hashInt;
        String string;
        for (int i = 0; i < strings.size(); i++) {
            string = strings.get(i);

            executor.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

                    // Simulate external data source network delay with sleep
                    Thread.sleep(100);

                    MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
                    hashBytes = md.digest(string.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
                    hashInt = getHashAsInteger(hashBytes);

                    synchronized (this) {
                        if (!hashExists.get(hashInt)) {
                            totalProcessingTime += System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
                            totalHashesProcessed++;

                            hashExists.set(hashInt);
                        }
                    }
                } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
                    logger.severe("MD5 algorithm not available");
                } finally {
                    latch.countDown();
                }
            });
        }

        latch.await();
    }

    public void printSummary() {
        double avgProcessingTime = totalHashesProcessed > 0 ? (double) totalProcessingTime / totalHashesProcessed : 0;
        System.out.println("Total unique hashes processed: " + hashExists.cardinality());
        System.out.println("Average processing time per hash: " + avgProcessingTime);
    }

    private int getHashAsInteger(byte[] hashBytes) {
        return ((hashBytes[0] & 0xFF) << 24) |
               ((hashBytes[1] & 0xFF) << 16) |
               ((hashBytes[2] & 0xFF) << 8 ) |
                (hashBytes[3] & 0xFF);
    }
}
```
In this code, each string is processed concurrently in its own thread. We use a BitSet to record the existence of each hash, and we use synchronization to ensure that only one thread can set a bit at any given time. This approach allows us to avoid contention on the hashExists BitSet, which would be problematic if multiple threads tried to set bits simultaneously.

We also compute a latency estimate by recording the start time when a string is submitted for processing and then calculating the difference when it finishes, which gives us an estimate of the processing time for that particular string. We add this to totalProcessingTime and increment totalHashesProcessed only if the hash does not already exist in our BitSet (to avoid duplicate hashes).

Finally, we use a CountDownLatch to ensure that we wait until all strings have been processed before returning from processBatch(). This allows us to keep track of how many strings are still being processed and prevents the method from returning prematurely. 

The summary report includes the total number of unique hashes processed and the average processing time per hash, which is computed by dividing totalProcessingTime by totalHashesProcessed. We use a logger to record any errors that might occur during the execution. 

This implementation makes trade-offs between efficiency (using BitSet for efficient memory usage), maintainability (encapsulating complexity in a clear and concise API), scalability (allowing concurrency with multiple threads, even under high load conditions), and usability (providing logging and metrics). The code is designed to be easily understood by junior developers.
']

