['```javascript
class GraphProcessor {
    constructor(dbClient, constraints) {
        this.db = dbClient; // Assume dbClient provides an interface to interact with a graph DB.
        this.constraints = constraints || {};
        this.isStreamingPaused = false; 
    }

    setConstraint(key, value) {
        if (this.constraints[key] && typeof this.constraints[key] === 'function') {
            return this.constraints[key](value); // Apply custom constraint check.
        } else {
            this.constraints[key] = value;
        }
    }

    pauseStreaming() {
        this.isStreamingPaused = true; 
    }

    resumeStreaming() {
        this.isStreamingPaused = false; 
    }

    async processData(data) {
        if (!this.db || !data || data.length === 0) return; // No database client or no data to process.

        while (this.isStreamingPaused) {
            await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 100)); // Pause for CPU optimization.
        }

        const batchSize = 100; // Example batch size.
        let batchedData = []; 
        try{
            data.forEach((item) => {
                if (batchedData.length < batchSize){
                    batchedData.push(item);
                } else {
                    this.db.addVertexAndEdgeBulk(batchedData); // Assume dbClient provides an interface to bulk add vertices and edges.
                    batchedData = []; 
                }
            });
        } catch (error) {
            console.error("Error processing data: ", error); // Basic error handling.
            if(this.constraints['retryPolicy'] && typeof this.constraints['retryPolicy'] === 'function'){
                return this.constraints['retryPolicy'](); 
            }
        }

    }
}
```
This is a JavaScript-based approach to the problem which involves creating a GraphProcessor class that can handle various constraints, pause/resume operations for CPU optimization and batch processing for efficient data handling in a graph database. The actual usage would depend on your specific requirements such as the available methods of interaction with the graph database.
']

['Your requirements involve writing large chunks of code that covers many aspects, from efficient data copying to parallel processing, error handling and logging, memory management, and more. However, it's also important to mention that JavaScript is primarily single-threaded and may not benefit fully from multithreading due to its event loop model.

Below is a simplified example of how you could start solving this problem, without considering all your points:

```javascript
function copyData(dataChunks, byteCount, checksum) {
    // Log the operation's start time
    console.time("copyData");

    let copiedBytes = 0;
    const buffer = Buffer.allocUnsafe(byteCount);

    for (let i = 0; i < dataChunks.length; ++i) {
        if (dataChunks[i].isCorrupted()) { // handle corrupted chunks
            console.warn("Detected corrupt chunk, skipping...");
            continue;
        }

        const chunkBytes = Math.min(byteCount - copiedBytes, dataChunks[i].length);
        dataChunks[i].copy(buffer, copiedBytes, 0, chunkBytes);

        copiedBytes += chunkBytes;
        if (copiedBytes >= byteCount) break; // stop if we've enough bytes
    }

    const checksumOk = buffer.checksum() === checksum; // validate the checksum
    if (!checksumOk) { 
        console.error("Checksum validation failed");
        throw new Error('Checksum validation failed');
    }

    // Log the operation's end time and return the buffer
    console.timeEnd("copyData");
    return buffer;
}
```
Please note that this is a highly simplified example, some of your constraints are beyond JavaScript's ability to handle efficiently without significant workarounds or additional tools. For instance, you mentioned a constrained environment with only 50MB of heap space allocated. In such environments, running Node.js and allocating memory in excess will crash the process due to V8's default memory limit (~1.7GB).

You should also consider using Web Workers for parallel processing when dealing with large amounts of data as it can offload computationally-heavy tasks onto separate threads, but this won't increase performance on a single-core system and will add additional complexity due to message passing between the main thread and workers. 

Remember that Node.js and JavaScript in general are not designed for high-performance CPU or memory workloads; they're more suitable for I/O-bound tasks such as web servers, streaming video and audio, and other event-driven scripts. If you need to perform extensive CPU or memory operations, it would be much better to use a lower-level language like C++.
