                $_SESSION[$cacheKey] = ['token' => $response['token'], 'expires' => time() + $response['expiresIn']];
                $success = true;
            } else {
                sleep($delay); // wait for the specified delay before retrying
                $attempts++;
                $delay *= 2; // exponential backoff: each attempt waits twice as long as the previous one
            }
        }
        if (!$success) {
            error_log("Failed to authenticate with external service after 5 attempts.");
            throw new Exception('External authentication service is currently unavailable. Please try again later.');
        }
    }
}
```
This code will cache the token and its expiration time in a session variable, so it does not need to call the external service on each request. The `callToExternalService()` function should be replaced with actual logic for calling your specific external authentication service. 

Remember that this is just a simple implementation, real-world applications would require much more comprehensive solutions and may involve using different strategies for managing sessions, caching, logging, error handling etc. Also, do not forget to secure sensitive data like passwords or tokens properly in accordance with security best practices.
