['Below is a simple implementation of such a function in C++. This code does not represent all possible aspects of your task, but it gives an idea of how you might structure your solution. It uses `std::chrono` library to manage timestamps and cooldown periods, and `<random>` for random attack selection.

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <random>

enum AttackType { DAMAGE, HEALING, COVER };
const int MIN_COOLDOWN = 5;
const int MAX_COOLDOWN = 15;
std::chrono::time_point<std::chrono::system_clock> lastAttackTime[3] = {
    std::chrono::system_clock::now(),
    std::chrono::system_clock::now(),
    std::chrono::system_clock::now()};
int health = 100;  // for example, you would use a service to get this value.

void selectAttack(std::mt19937& gen) {
  int cooldown = MIN_COOLDOWN + (gen() % (MAX_COOLDOWN - MIN_COOLDOWN + 1));

  std::vector<AttackType> possibleAttacks;
  if (health < 30) {
    // If health is low, prioritize healing attacks.
    possibleAttacks = {HEALING};
  } else {
    // Otherwise, include damage and cover attacks.
    possibleAttacks = {DAMAGE, COVER};
  }

  std::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, possibleAttacks.size() - 1);
  AttackType chosenAttack;

  auto currentTime = std::chrono::system_clock::now();
  if (currentTime - lastAttackTime[possibleAttacks[dis(gen)]] > std::chrono::seconds(cooldown)) {
    // If cooldown has passed, select a random attack type.
    chosenAttack = possibleAttacks[dis(gen)];
  } else if (possibleAttacks.size() > 1) {
    // Otherwise, select the other attack type.
    chosenAttack = (chosenAttack == possibleAttacks[0]) ? possibleAttacks[1] : possibleAttacks[0];
  }

  lastAttackTime[chosenAttack] = currentTime;
  std::cout << "Selected attack: " << chosenAttack << '\n';
}
```

This function first selects a random cooldown period between MIN_COOLDOWN and MAX_COOLDOWN. Then, it creates a list of possible attacks based on the character's health status. It then randomly selects an attack from this list (prioritizing healing if the health is low). If the chosen attack's cooldown has not yet passed, it selects another random attack type. Finally, it updates the last time the selected attack was used and outputs the chosen attack type to the console. 

This code does not handle the integration with external services or the network latency issue. You would need to use appropriate APIs for this, which depends on your specific setup and requirements. 

Also note that managing game modes, stamina system, fallback mechanism for service unavailability is beyond the scope of this function and needs to be handled separately depending on the actual context and specifications provided by the game designer or project manager.
']

