['Here is an example implementation using `nlohmann/json` for parsing JSON in C++ and `csv-parser` for writing CSV files. This sample uses a JSON array as input where each item represents one user activity. The output CSV file will have the headers 'userId', 'timestamp', 'activityType' and 'duration_min'. Malformed entries are skipped, and you can check how many valid entries were processed and their average size in the `stats.log` log file.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include "json.hpp" // You need to install this using vcpkg or any other package manager
#include "csv-parser/parser.hpp"

using json = nlohmann::json;

int main() {
    std::ifstream i("input.json");
    std::ofstream o("output.csv");
    std::ofstream s("stats.log", std::ios_base::app); // Append to log file

    json j;
    size_t validEntries = 0, totalSize = 0;
    o << "userId" << ',' << "timestamp" << ',' 
      << "activityType" << ',' << "duration_min" << '\n';

    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(i, line)) {
        try {
            j = json::parse(line);

            // Check if the parsed JSON object has all necessary fields
            if (!j.contains("userId") || !j.contains("timestamp") || 
                !j.contains("activityType") || !j.contains("duration")) 
            {
                s << "Malformed entry skipped: Missing one or more fields.\n";
                continue;
            }

            // Write the parsed data to CSV file, rounding duration to nearest integer
            o << j["userId"].get<std::string>() << ',' 
              << j["timestamp"].get<int64_t>() << ',' 
              << j["activityType"].get<std::string>() << ','
              << std::round(j["duration"].get<double>() / 60.0) << '\n'; // Converting seconds to minutes

            validEntries++;
            totalSize += line.size();
        } catch (json::parse_error& e) {
            s << "Malformed entry skipped: " << e.what() << "\n";
        }
    }

    s << "Valid entries processed: " << validEntries << '\n';
    if(validEntries > 0) 
      s << "Average entry size: " << totalSize / (double)validEntries << '\n';

    i.close();
    o.close();
    s.close();

    return 0;
}
```
This program has a time complexity of O(n) as it processes each line in the JSON file only once, and a space complexity of O(1), since it does not use any additional data structures that grow with input size.

Make sure to install `json` by using vcpkg or any other package manager. If you are using Visual Studio Code, the C/C++ extension can help auto-complete the code. Include paths may need to be adjusted based on your setup. 

