```
Then we'll create a function to create a new structure with randomly set `CRCFLAG`. We will use the rand crate for this:

```rust
use rand::Rng;

fn create_structure() -> NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    let mut rng = rand::thread_rng();
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
        RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG: rng.gen(),
        RNTI: rng.gen(),
        CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG: format!!("R{}", rng.gen::<u32>()),
        CRCFLAG: rng.gen(),
    }
}
```
The next part is creating a high-performance memory pool using crossbeam's `SegQueue` for lock-free queues and Tokio's asynchronous executor to perform tasks in parallel:

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use crossbeam_utils::SegQueue;
use tokio::task::JoinHandle;
use tokio::runtime::Runtime;
use rand::Rng;

async fn async_create_structure(pool: Arc<SegQueue<NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T>>) {
    let _ = pool.push(create_structure());
}

fn main() {
    let num_structs = 10; // Number of structures to create

    let (tx, rx) = crossbeam_utils::seg_queue::unbounded();

    let pool = Arc::new(rx);
    let mut handles: Vec<JoinHandle<()>> = vec![];

    for _ in 0..num_structs {
        let rt = Runtime::new().unwrap();
        handles.push(rt.spawn(async move {
            async_create_structure(pool.clone()).await;
        }));
    }

    joinall(&handles).await;
}
```
Please note that this is a simplified example and doesn't cover all of your requirements, but it should give you a good starting point. You'll need to add error handling, command-line argument parsing, custom allocators for contiguous and non-contiguous memory allocation patterns, integration with an external configuration management system, tracking memory usage statistics, data processing pipeline, fault tolerance, scalability and other features. The complexity of your program depends on the specific requirements you have, so it's hard to provide a complete solution in one go.
