Please note that this is a basic implementation and may need to be adjusted based on specifics of your project, such as data structures, file paths, error logging mechanism etc. Also it does not include any actual I/O operations for reading the configuration file or outputting warnings or error logs. Those functionalities would depend on your specific environment and requirements:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#define MAX_ENTRIES 10000
typedef struct NETWORK_LAYER_EVENT {
    int EVENT_ID;
    time_t TIMESTAMP;
    int STATUS_CODE;
} NETWORK_LAYER_EVENT_T;

NETWORK_LAYER_EVENT_T* processAndValidateConfigurationFile(const char *fileName, struct tm minTimestamp, 
                                                          struct tm maxTimestamp, const int validCodes[], size_t n) {
    NETWORK_LAYER_EVENT_T *events = malloc(MAX_ENTRIES * sizeof(*events));
    if (events == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    FILE *file = fopen(fileName, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open file\n");
        free(events);
        return NULL;
    }

    char line[256];
    int entryCount = 0;
    while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), file)) {
        if (entryCount >= MAX_ENTRIES) {
            printf("Maximum entries limit reached\n");
            break;
        }

        NETWORK_LAYER_EVENT_T event;
        int itemsRead = sscanf(line, "%d %ld %d", &event.EVENT_ID, &event.TIMESTAMP, &event.STATUS_CODE);
        if (itemsRead != 3) {
            printf("Malformed entry: %s\n", line);
            continue;
        }

        struct tm *timestamp = localtime(&event.TIMESTAMP);
        char timestampBuffer[11];
        strftime(timestampBuffer, sizeof(timestampBuffer), "%Y-%m-%d", timestamp);
        if (strptime(timestampBuffer, "%F", timestamp) == NULL ||
            mktime(minTimestamp) > event.TIMESTAMP ||
            event.TIMESTAMP >= mktime(&maxTimestamp)) {
            printf("Invalid TIMESTAMP: %s\n", line);
        } else if (bsearch(&event.STATUS_CODE, validCodes, n, sizeof(*validCodes), compare) == NULL) {
            printf("Invalid STATUS_CODE: %d for entry %s\n", event.STATUS_CODE, line);
        } else {
            events[entryCount++] = event;
        }
    }

    fclose(file);
    return events;
}
```
This function reads the file line by line and validates each entry as it is read. If an error occurs while reading or parsing a line, it prints an appropriate message to `stdout` (or logs in your actual environment) and continues with the next line. It also checks for buffer overflows/underflows during reading operations using `fgets()` function. 

Remember that you need to free allocated memory once it's not needed anymore:
```c
free(events);
```
Also, don't forget to check return value of `processAndValidateConfigurationFile()` call in your code to handle potential errors. If it returns NULL, it means some error occurred during file processing (for example, memory allocation failure). 

This function also checks if the sequence number of the last successfully parsed entry using Pythonic exception-handling techniques:
```c
if (itemsRead != 3) {
    printf("Malformed entry: %s\n", line);
    continue;
}
```
If `itemsRead` is not 3, it means that an error occurred during parsing a line and we skip this entry. The sequence number of the last successfully parsed entry can be obtained by tracking the value of `entryCount` after successful parsing or skipping of entries. In other words, if the line was malformed and could not be parsed correctly, `entryCount` won't be updated for that line.
']

