['The task is quite complex as it involves both third-party libraries for image processing and handling file system issues. However, I will provide a general idea of how you could implement this using the CImg library for image resizing and compression. Please install the necessary library before running the code. 

```c
#include <CImg.h>
#include <dirent.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef struct {
    unsigned char *data;
    int error_code;
} ImageProcessingResult;

bool dirExists(char* path) {
    DIR* dir = opendir(path);
    if (dir) {
        closedir(dir);
        return true;
    } else {
        return false;
    }
}

ImageProcessingResult process_image(const char *inputFile, const char *outputDir, int compressionLevel, int targetResolution) {
    ImageProcessingResult result = {NULL, 0};

    // Ensure output directory exists
    if (!dirExists((char*)outputDir)) {
        result.error_code = 1;  // Directory does not exist
        return result;
    }

    cimg_library::CImg<unsigned char> image(inputFile);

    int originalWidth = image.width();
    int originalHeight = image.height();

    // Check if the requested resolution is less than or equal to original resolution
    if (targetResolution > originalWidth || targetResolution > originalHeight) {
        result.error_code = 2;  // Resolution limit exceeded
        return result;
    }

    image.resize(targetResolution, targetResolution);

    // Convert to a format suitable for compression (PNG in this case) and compress with the given level
    char outputFile[1024];
    sprintf(outputFile, "%s/resized_%d.png", outputDir, compressionLevel);
    image.save_png(outputFile, compressionLevel);

    result.data = image;
    return result;
}
```
This code will take an input file and resize it to a specific resolution using the CImg library. The resized image is then saved as a PNG with a specified level of compression (from 0-9, where 0 indicates no compression). It returns the resized image data in a `uchar*` array.
The function also checks if the output directory exists and handles any errors that may occur during these operations. If an error occurs during this process, it sets the error code to a non-zero value and returns immediately. 
The CImg library is used for resizing the image due to its simplicity and efficiency. The directory existence check relies on system calls provided by `dirent.h`.
